"Full accounting for verifiable outsourcing (https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/242)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/242\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 71\nL0: # What a lovely hat\nL1: #### Is it made out of 【0†tin foil†iacr.org】?\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Cryptology ePrint Archive】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【2†Papers】 Updates from the last:\nL6:     * 【3†7 days】\nL7:     * 【4†31 days】\nL8:     * 【5†6 months】\nL9:     * 【6†365 days】\nL10:     * * * *\nL11: \nL12:     * 【7†Listing by year】\nL13:     * 【8†All papers】\nL14:     * 【9†Compact view】\nL15:     * 【10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org】\nL16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * 【11†How to cite】\nL19:     * * * *\nL20:     * 【12†Harvesting metadata】\nL21:   * 【2†Submissions】\nL22:     * 【13†Submit a paper】\nL23:     * 【14†Revise or withdraw a paper】\nL24:     * 【15†Acceptance and publishing conditions】\nL25: \nL26: 【16†Image: Search Button】\nL27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2017/242\nL30: \nL31: ### 【17†Full accounting for verifiable outsourcing】\nL32: \nL33: Riad S. Wahby, Ye Ji, Andrew J. Blumberg, abhi shelat, Justin Thaler, Michael Walfish, and Thomas Wies\nL34: ##### Abstract\nL35: Systems for verifiable outsourcing incur costs for a prover, a verifier, and precomputation; outsourcing makes sense when the combination of these costs is cheaper than not outsourcing. Yet, when prior works impose quantitative thresholds to analyze whether outsourcing is justified, they generally ignore prover costs. Verifiable ASICs (VA)---in which the prover is a custom chip---is the other way around: its cost calculations ignore precomputation.\nL36: This paper describes a new VA system, called Giraffe; charges Giraffe for all three costs; and identifies regimes where outsourcing is worthwhile. Giraffe’s base is an interactive proof geared to data-parallel computation. Giraffe makes this protocol asymptotically optimal for the prover and improves the verifier's main bottleneck by almost 3x, both of which are of independent interest.\nL37: Giraffe also develops a design template that produces hardware designs automatically for a wide range of parameters, introduces hardware primitives molded to the protocol’s data flows, and incorporates program analyses that expand applicability. Giraffe wins even when outsourcing several tens of sub-computations, scales to 500x larger computations than prior work, and can profitably outsource parts of programs that are not worthwhile to outsource in full.\nL38: ##### Metadata\nL39: Available format(s)\nL40:     【17†PDF】\nL41: Category\nL42:     【18†Implementation】\nL43: Publication info\nL44:     Published elsewhere. Major revision. CCS 2017\nL45: DOI\nL46:     【19†10.1145/3133956.3133984†doi.org】\nL47: Keywords\nL48:     【20†verifiable computation】【21†trustworthy hardware】【22†probabilistic proofs】【23†interactive proofs】【24†sum-check protocol】\nL49: Contact author(s)\nL50:     rsw @ cs stanford edu\nL51: History\nL52:     2017-09-05: last of 5 revisions\nL53:     2017-03-14: received\nL54:     【25†See all versions】\nL55: Short URL\nL56:     【26†https://ia.cr/2017/242†ia.cr】\nL57: License\nL58: \nL59: 【27†CC BY†creativecommons.org】\nL60: BibTeX 【28†Image】Copy to clipboard\nL61: \nL62:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2017/242,\nL63:           author = {Riad S.  Wahby and Ye Ji and Andrew J.  Blumberg and abhi shelat and Justin Thaler and Michael Walfish and Thomas Wies},\nL64:           title = {Full accounting for verifiable outsourcing},\nL65:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2017/242},\nL66:           year = {2017},\nL67:           doi = {10.1145/3133956.3133984},\nL68:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2017/242}\nL69:     }\nL70: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.pepper-project.org/giraffe-ccs17.pdf)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 16; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.pepper-project.org/giraffe-ccs17.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2083\nL0@P0: Full Accounting for Verifiable Outsourcing\nL1@P0: Riad S. Wahby⋆ Ye Ji◦ Andrew J. Blumberg†\nL2@P0: abhi shelat‡Justin Thaler△ Michael Walfish◦ Thomas Wies◦\nL3@P0: ⋆Stanford ◦NYU †UT Austin ‡Northeastern △Georgetown\nL4@P0: ABSTRACT\nL5@P0: Systems for verifiable outsourcing incur costs for a prover, a verifier,\nL6@P0: and precomputation; outsourcing makes sense when the combi\u0002nation of these costs is cheaper than not outsourcing. Yet, when\nL7@P0: prior works impose quantitative thresholds to analyze whether out\u0002sourcing is justified, they generally ignore prover costs. Verifiable\nL8@P0: ASICs (VA)—in which the prover is a custom chip—is the other way\nL9@P0: around: its cost calculations ignore precomputation.\nL10@P0: This paper describes a new VA system, called Giraffe; charges\nL11@P0: Giraffe for all three costs; and identifies regimes where outsourcing\nL12@P0: is worthwhile. Giraffe’s base is an interactive proof geared to data\u0002parallel computation. Giraffe makes this protocol asymptotically\nL13@P0: optimal for the prover and improves the verifier’s main bottleneck\nL14@P0: by almost 3×, both of which are of independent interest. Giraffe\nL15@P0: also develops a design template that produces hardware designs\nL16@P0: automatically for a wide range of parameters, introduces hardware\nL17@P0: primitives molded to the protocol’s data flows, and incorporates\nL18@P0: program analyses that expand applicability. Giraffe wins even when\nL19@P0: outsourcing several tens of sub-computations, scales to 500× larger\nL20@P0: computations than prior work, and can profitably outsource parts\nL21@P0: of programs that are not worthwhile to outsource in full.\nL22@P0: 1 INTRODUCTION\nL23@P0: In probabilistic proofs—Interactive Proofs (IPs) [12, 49, 50, 58, 76],\nL24@P0: arguments [30, 52, 54, 62], SNARGs [48], SNARKs [26, 47], and\nL25@P0: PCPs [9, 10]—a prover efficiently convinces a verifier of a claim, in\nL26@P0: such a way that the verifier is highly likely to reject a false claim.\nL27@P0: These protocols are foundational in complexity theory and cryptog\u0002raphy. There has also been substantial progress in implementations\nL28@P0: over the last six years [14, 15, 17–19, 21, 22, 32, 34, 36, 37, 39, 40, 42,\nL29@P0: 45, 47, 56, 64, 66, 72–75, 77, 79, 81, 82, 84, 88] (for a survey, see [85]),\nL30@P0: based on theoretical refinements and systems work.\nL31@P0: A central application example is verifiable outsourcing. The veri\u0002fier specifies a computation and input; the prover returns the (pur\u0002ported) output and proves the claim that “the returned output equals\nL32@P0: the computation applied to the input.” The essential property here\nL33@P0: is that the verifier’s probabilistic checks are asymptotically less\nL34@P0: expensive than executing the computation; as a result, outsourcing\nL35@P0: Permission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for personal or\nL36@P0: classroom use is granted without fee provided that copies are not made or distributed\nL37@P0: for profit or commercial advantage and that copies bear this notice and the full citation\nL38@P0: on the first page. Copyrights for components of this work owned by others than the\nL39@P0: author(s) must be honored. Abstracting with credit is permitted. To copy otherwise, or\nL40@P0: republish, to post on servers or to redistribute to lists, requires prior specific permission\nL41@P0: and/or a fee. Request permissions from permissions@acm.org.\nL42@P0: CCS’17, Oct. 30–Nov. 3, 2017, Dallas, TX, USA.\nL43@P0: © 2017 Copyright held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to Associa\u0002tion for Computing Machinery.\nL44@P0: ACM ISBN ISBN 978-1-4503-4946-8/17/10. . . $15.00\nL45@P0: https://doi.org/10.1145/3133956.3133984\nL46@P0: can be worthwhile for the verifier. This picture partially motivated\nL47@P0: the original theory [13, 46, 49, 62] and has reappeared in tales of\nL48@P0: outsourcing to the cloud. But to validate these stories, one must\nL49@P0: consider three kinds of costs:\nL50@P0: • Prover overhead. Even in the best general-purpose probabilistic\nL51@P0: proof protocols, the prover has enormous overhead in running\nL52@P0: the protocol versus simply executing the underlying computa\u0002tion: the ratio between these is typically at least 107\nL53@P0: [85, Fig. 5].\nL54@P0: • Precomputation. Many of the implemented protocols require a\nL55@P0: setup phase, performed by the verifier or a party that the verifier\nL56@P0: trusts. This phase is required for each computation and can be\nL57@P0: reused over different input-output instances. Its costs are usually\nL58@P0: proportional to the time to run the computation. (Precomputation\nL59@P0: can be asymptotically suppressed or even eliminated, but at vastly\nL60@P0: higher concrete cost [17, 21, 22, 34]; see §10.)\nL61@P0: • Verifier overhead. Separate from precomputation, there are inher\u0002ent costs that the verifier incurs for each input-output instance.\nL62@P0: These costs are at least linear in the input and output lengths.\nL63@P0: More or less tacitly, “practical” work in this area has bundled in\nL64@P0: assumptions about the regimes in which these costs are reasonable\nL65@P0: for the operator of the verifier.1 For example, one way to tame the\nL66@P0: costs is not to charge the operator for precomputation. This is the\nL67@P0: approach taken in Pinocchio, which focuses on per-instance verifier\nL68@P0: overhead [66, 67].2 This choice can be justified if there is a trusted\nL69@P0: third party with extremely inexpensive cycles.\nL70@P0: Another possibility is to target data-parallel computations, mean\u0002ing identical sub-computations on different inputs. Here, one can\nL71@P0: charge the operator of the verifier for the precomputation, which\nL72@P0: amortizes, and then identify cross-over points where the verifier\nL73@P0: saves work from outsourcing [32, 37, 66, 73–75, 81, 84, 88].\nL74@P0: In both of these cases, prover overhead is measured but in some\nL75@P0: sense ignored (when considering whether outsourcing is worth\u0002while). This would make sense if the prover’s cycles were vastly\nL76@P0: cheaper than the verifier’s—the required ratio is approximately the\nL77@P0: prover’s overhead: 107×—or if the outsourced computation could\nL78@P0: not be executed in any other way.\nL79@P0: Recently, Zebra [82] used a different justification by observing\nL80@P0: that one can gain high-assurance execution of custom chips (ASICs)\nL81@P0: by using trusted slow chips to verify the outputs of untrusted fast\nL82@P0: chips. In this Verifiable ASICs (VA) domain (§2.3), one can charge\nL83@P0: 1A variant of this story, exploiting the zero knowledge property of some probabilistic\nL84@P0: proofs, such as SNARKs [26, 47], includes applications where the proof can incorporate\nL85@P0: input hidden from the verifier [18, 39, 64, 66]. Here, one does not obsess over the\nL86@P0: verifier’s overhead or total cost comparisons because the verifier cannot execute locally.\nL87@P0: Nevertheless, identifying regimes where overhead is reasonable similarly requires\nL88@P0: some effort. We do not discuss in detail, but see §9 and §10.\nL89@P0-1: 2Pinocchio certainly considers precomputation [66, §5.3], but its emphasized compari\u0002son is between native execution and verifier overhead.the operator for both verifier and prover and still identify regimes\nL90@P1: where their combination outperforms a baseline of executing the\nL91@P1: functionality in a trusted slow chip. However, Zebra does not charge\nL92@P1: for precomputation—and worse, introduces a preposterous assump\u0002tion about daily delivery of hard drives to handle the problem.\nL93@P1: The work of this paper is to create a system, Giraffe; to charge the\nL94@P1: operator for all three costs; and to seek out regimes where this com\u0002bined cost is superior to the baseline. Giraffe builds on Zebra and\nL95@P1: likewise targets the VA setting. However, some of Giraffe’s results\nL96@P1: and techniques apply to verifiable outsourcing more generally.\nL97@P1: Giraffe has two high-level aspects. The first is a new probabilistic\nL98@P1: proof built on a protocol that we call T13 [77, §7]. As with all work\nL99@P1: in this area, T13 requires computations to be expressed as arithmetic\nL100@P1: circuits, or ACs (§2.1). T13 has three key advantages: (a) T13 is a\nL101@P1: variant of CMT [36, 49], which is Zebra’s base, and thus promises\nL102@P1: amenability to hardware implementation; (b) in the VA context, T13\nL103@P1: can in principle pay for precomputation and break even, because it is\nL104@P1: geared to the aforementioned data-parallel model: precomputation\nL105@P1: is proportional to one sub-computation, and amortizes over N sub\u0002computations; and (c) T13 ought to permit breaking even for small\nL106@P1: N: CMT has low overhead compared to alternatives [85]. From this\nL107@P1: starting point, Giraffe does the following (§3):\nL108@P1: • Giraffe improves T13. Most significantly, Giraffe makes the prover\nL109@P1: asymptotically time-optimal: for sufficiently large N, the prover’s\nL110@P1: work is now only a multiple (≈ 10×) of executing the AC (§3.1).\nL111@P1: This can save an order of magnitude or more for any implemen\u0002tation of T13 in any context (for example, vSQL [88]), and is of\nL112@P1: independent interest.\nL113@P1: • Giraffe develops a design template that automatically instantiates\nL114@P1: physically realizable, efficient, high-throughput ASIC designs for\nL115@P1: the prover and verifier. The basic challenge is that, consistent\nL116@P1: with our search for applicable regimes, there are variables with\nL117@P1: wide ranges: small and large N, different hardware substrates,\nL118@P1: etc. As a result, the optimal architectures are diverse. For ex\u0002ample, large ACs (large sub-computations and/or large N) must\nL119@P1: iteratively reuse the underlying hardware whereas small ACs\nL120@P1: call for high parallelism. Giraffe responds with the RWSR: a new\nL121@P1: hardware structure that, when applied to the data flows in T13,\nL122@P1: both runs efficiently in serial execution and parallelizes easily.\nL123@P1: • Giraffe demonstrates algorithmic improvements that apply to all\nL124@P1: CMT-based systems [36, 77, 79, 81, 82, 88]. This includes reducing\nL125@P1: the verifier’s main bottleneck by ≈3× (§3.3), eliminating a log\nL126@P1: factor from one of the verifier’s other computations by shifting\nL127@P1: additional work to the prover, and other optimizations that result\nL128@P1: in constant-factor improvements ([83, Appx. B.2]).\nL129@P1: The second aspect of Giraffe is motivated by our search for\nL130@P1: applicable regimes. In existing systems, protocol overhead limits\nL131@P1: the maximum size of a computation that can be outsourced. Worse,\nL132@P1: outsourcing really makes sense only if the computation is naturally\nL133@P1: expressed as an AC; otherwise, the asymptotic savings do not apply\nL134@P1: until program sizes are well beyond the aforementioned maximum.\nL135@P1: While these systems differ in the particulars, their restrictions are\nL136@P1: qualitatively similar—and there has been no fundamental progress\nL137@P1: on the expressivity issue over the last six years. As a consequence, it\nL138@P1: seems imperative to adapt to this situation. Two possible approaches\nL139@P1: are to handle these constraints by outsourcing amenable pieces\nL140@P1: of a given computation and to apply program transformations\nL141@P1: to increase the range of suitable computations. These ideas have\nL142@P1: of course appeared in the literature on compiling cryptographic\nL143@P1: protocols [43, 45, 59, 87], but previous efforts in the context of\nL144@P1: verifiable outsourcing have been very limited [37, 84].\nL145@P1: We study techniques for each of these approaches, adapted to\nL146@P1: this setting (§4). Giraffe employs slicing, which takes as input an ab\u0002stract cost model and a program, automatically identifies amenable\nL147@P1: subregions of the program, and generates glue code to sew the out\u0002sourced pieces into the rest of the program. Slicing is a very general\nL148@P1: technique that can work with all probabilistic proof implementa\u0002tions. Giraffe also uses squashing, which transforms sequential ACs\nL149@P1: into parallel ACs and adjusts the verifier to link these computations;\nL150@P1: this is relevant to CMT and T13, which require parallel ACs.\nL151@P1: Our implementation of Giraffe (§5) applies the above transforma\u0002tions to programs written in a subset of C, producing one or more\nL152@P1: ACs. Giraffe’s design template uses these ACs, along with several\nL153@P1: physical parameters (hardware substrates, chip area, etc.), and auto\u0002matically generates concrete hardware designs for the prover and\nL154@P1: verifier, built in SystemVerilog, that can be used for cycle-accurate\nL155@P1: simulation or synthesized (i.e., compiled to a chip).\nL156@P1: We evaluate using detailed simulation and modeling of these\nL157@P1: generated hardware designs. Accounting for all costs (prover, pre\u0002computation, verifier), Giraffe saves compared to native execu\u0002tion across a wide range of computation sizes and hardware sub\u0002strates (§6.2). Giraffe breaks even on operating costs for N≈30 par\u0002allel sub-computations; this value is essentially independent of the\nL158@P1: size of each sub-computation (§6.1). Compared to prior work in the\nL159@P1: VA setting, Giraffe scales to 500× larger computation sizes, holding\nL160@P1: all else constant (§8.1). A disadvantage of Giraffe is that its verifier is\nL161@P1: costlier than Zebra’s, and thus Giraffe’s break-even point is higher\nL162@P1: than Zebra’s. This is not because Zebra is fundamentally cheaper,\nL163@P1: but rather because it assumes away precomputation and thus does\nL164@P1: not have to pay for it. Furthermore, Giraffe’s program analysis\nL165@P1: techniques expand applicability beyond Zebra; our experiments\nL166@P1: demonstrate that slicing enables an image-matching application\nL167@P1: that neither Zebra nor Giraffe could otherwise handle (§8.2).\nL168@P1: Nevertheless, Giraffe has limitations (some of which reflect the\nL169@P1: research area itself (§9)). Breaking even requires data-parallel com\u0002putations (to amortize precomputation), requires that the computa\u0002tion be naturally expressed as a layered AC, and requires a large gap\nL170@P1: between the hardware technologies used for the verifier and prover\nL171@P1: (which holds in some concrete settings; see [82, §1]). Moreover, the\nL172@P1: absolute cost of verifiability is still very high. Finally, the program\nL173@P1: transformation techniques have taken only a small first step.\nL174@P1: Despite these limitations, we think that Giraffe has a substantial\nL175@P1: claim to significance: it adopts the most stringent cost regime in\nL176@P1: the verifiable outsourcing literature and (to our knowledge) is the\nL177@P1: only system that can profitably outsource under this accounting.\nL178@P1: Debts and contributions. Giraffe builds on the T13 protocol [77,\nL179@P1: §7] and an optimization [78] (§2.2). It also generalizes a prior tech\u0002nique [1–3, 77, 82] (§3.2, “Algorithm”). Finally, Giraffe borrows from\nL180@P1: Zebra [82], specifically: the setting (§2.3), how to evaluate in that\nL181@P1-2: setting (§2.3, §6.2), a high-level design strategy (implicit in thispaper), a design for a module within the prover (footnote 5), and\nL182@P2: the application to Curve25519 (§8.1). Giraffe’s contributions are:\nL183@P2: • Algorithmic refinements of the T13 interactive proof, yielding\nL184@P2: an asymptotically optimal prover (§3.1) and a ≈3× reduction in\nL185@P2: the verifier’s main bottleneck (§3.3).\nL186@P2: • Hardware design templates for prover and verifier chips (§3.2,\nL187@P2: “Computing in hardware”; §3.3). We note that automatically gener\u0002ating a wide variety of optimized hardware designs is a significant\nL188@P2: technical challenge; it is achieved here via the introduction of\nL189@P2: the RWSR (and other hardware primitives), and the observation\nL190@P2: that RWSRs service a wide range of possible designs.\nL191@P2: • Techniques for compiling from a subset of C to ACs while auto\u0002matically optimizing for outsourcing based on cost models (§4).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - pepper-project/giraffe: An implementation of \"Full accounting for verifiable outsourcing\" (CCS17) · GitHub (https://github.com/pepper-project/giraffe)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/pepper-project/giraffe\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 230\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your code as you build】